[{:db/id 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a278ff29-60c3-57db-888f-d20c4575a799"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092079 :question/problem "If ![](images/38/38d451c4fe8eff5b96fe2df730315c129fac48c05324c8603576a69898665e54.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/57/576b0cfcb7e902b8fcb9c98d25a2145f760618d9a56ed257f4828d6ab3b05064.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=-5x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/1e/1e196ae1de5d8582ec93c6f964187b9b2b85b54f3b23e4017e50a1545310348c.svg)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/67/67e700524b8c7acc8923fd97acd1bb77b391944b91c891e4ce9388b22575709b.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092078 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of ![](images/39/398028421c4e26ae608c211073d010cfd7860476cec0861cb986bcc7a9cc91af.svg)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/10/105c85a9f0f6bc92ac8f062d52790babc3331db1283bf4380fad442418d870eb.svg)\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/82/82cec52e485c08116a7e07f4b9d5b01355e8bebdd4fb910535a001e5e979a099.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b7043291-4c2d-5218-b2ec-199ed93eadcb" "question-ba90f51b-352b-5787-ba63-3059791890ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092080 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n![](images/b1/b1b6bc95f7d29e0c64007e7c19a4690eb3def3e58d677d8a21604315bbb21c0a.svg)\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/81/812c2f9dfcb3943cc13c47d2f1b85d9b1a67ce10c9e6a965a9bb915655d0316c.svg)\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](images/7d/7d3a9e02893b98a2739398d48138be4c930ea87b3e3577776cfdd7edf6faf4e7.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/ea/ea5ff5d281eb5376520b2763f8dd96a926d52b6485452dd60b741b48ad0d7def.svg)\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](images/db/db85af375c67eca1f1d197a284b99ef7baf9fdb543b376c27e02c3d837742b68.svg)\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n![](images/a8/a84725763b84c77f8ce4d4954fe125d5b30f735649769e1d87e700287f1c1ff6.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a278ff29-60c3-57db-888f-d20c4575a799" :question/id #uuid "a278ff29-60c3-57db-888f-d20c4575a799" :question/math-academy-id "q-36763" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =0.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-354dd761-ee66-534a-8529-b3848bd7316c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-354dd761-ee66-534a-8529-b3848bd7316c" :answer-field/id #uuid "354dd761-ee66-534a-8529-b3848bd7316c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93521080-c7bb-5183-8fdf-d1ef0f91ea9a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-152f16d1-7717-52d3-80d1-4e93f7e18085" "answer-f1063d93-3165-53f2-b0c5-7b6d7f584b7e" "answer-7b33cfbb-3ab3-5640-a98a-1b640acd1fa3" "answer-ae8b7370-66aa-59a2-8b42-8c520344571e" "answer-93521080-c7bb-5183-8fdf-d1ef0f91ea9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-152f16d1-7717-52d3-80d1-4e93f7e18085" :answer/id #uuid "152f16d1-7717-52d3-80d1-4e93f7e18085" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1063d93-3165-53f2-b0c5-7b6d7f584b7e" :answer/id #uuid "f1063d93-3165-53f2-b0c5-7b6d7f584b7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b33cfbb-3ab3-5640-a98a-1b640acd1fa3" :answer/id #uuid "7b33cfbb-3ab3-5640-a98a-1b640acd1fa3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae8b7370-66aa-59a2-8b42-8c520344571e" :answer/id #uuid "ae8b7370-66aa-59a2-8b42-8c520344571e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93521080-c7bb-5183-8fdf-d1ef0f91ea9a" :answer/id #uuid "93521080-c7bb-5183-8fdf-d1ef0f91ea9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186357567 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186393996 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =10.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186348360 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =6.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b7043291-4c2d-5218-b2ec-199ed93eadcb" :question/id #uuid "b7043291-4c2d-5218-b2ec-199ed93eadcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107275" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =8$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-(-5)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3f81bcb6-7113-5591-b120-34386b2b4833"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3f81bcb6-7113-5591-b120-34386b2b4833" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f81bcb6-7113-5591-b120-34386b2b4833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5abf6ee9-af9e-57ac-8483-5a3e973653e7" "answer-e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" "answer-69549ed7-5e3c-59e7-9843-4f93cd72fb1b" "answer-07c3b6eb-d05a-563e-9054-1a09875cbcca" "answer-22e10d12-85c5-5f27-8869-ab11e85fc4ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5abf6ee9-af9e-57ac-8483-5a3e973653e7" :answer/id #uuid "5abf6ee9-af9e-57ac-8483-5a3e973653e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" :answer/id #uuid "e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69549ed7-5e3c-59e7-9843-4f93cd72fb1b" :answer/id #uuid "69549ed7-5e3c-59e7-9843-4f93cd72fb1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07c3b6eb-d05a-563e-9054-1a09875cbcca" :answer/id #uuid "07c3b6eb-d05a-563e-9054-1a09875cbcca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22e10d12-85c5-5f27-8869-ab11e85fc4ce" :answer/id #uuid "22e10d12-85c5-5f27-8869-ab11e85fc4ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ba90f51b-352b-5787-ba63-3059791890ac" :question/id #uuid "ba90f51b-352b-5787-ba63-3059791890ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-80326" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =11$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-11[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1754e42f-ef75-5680-942c-adf751ac0ab2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1754e42f-ef75-5680-942c-adf751ac0ab2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1754e42f-ef75-5680-942c-adf751ac0ab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee78a4db-270a-5a24-94aa-7fd979241cf9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-48e3bd55-0f69-5377-b778-7910fd7ee930" "answer-0e2a0fd5-5d0b-5043-bea5-3d7c9cdc5bec" "answer-4508942a-d2b4-51d0-a2a4-1cfbb3d6e36f" "answer-ee78a4db-270a-5a24-94aa-7fd979241cf9" "answer-9624a80a-ce12-59e9-9acd-2860974a182f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48e3bd55-0f69-5377-b778-7910fd7ee930" :answer/id #uuid "48e3bd55-0f69-5377-b778-7910fd7ee930" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e2a0fd5-5d0b-5043-bea5-3d7c9cdc5bec" :answer/id #uuid "0e2a0fd5-5d0b-5043-bea5-3d7c9cdc5bec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4508942a-d2b4-51d0-a2a4-1cfbb3d6e36f" :answer/id #uuid "4508942a-d2b4-51d0-a2a4-1cfbb3d6e36f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee78a4db-270a-5a24-94aa-7fd979241cf9" :answer/id #uuid "ee78a4db-270a-5a24-94aa-7fd979241cf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9624a80a-ce12-59e9-9acd-2860974a182f" :answer/id #uuid "9624a80a-ce12-59e9-9acd-2860974a182f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093440 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix ![](images/30/30880937eed54b00cb0ebe13bd2a46e2634e4193343b1db552b30836ed1e055b.svg) Given that $\\lambda=3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ how can we find an eigenvector that corresponds to this eigenvalue?\n\nRecall that a non-zero vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ with corresponding eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if \n\n$$\nA{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}={\\color{red}\\lambda}{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this equation, substituting ${\\color{red}{\\lambda = 3}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =3v \\\\ Av-3v & =0 \\\\ Av-3Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-3I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to $\\lambda = {\\color{red}3},$ we need to find a non-zero solution of the equation \n\n$$\n(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A-{\\color{red}3}I$ is already in row echelon form, and it has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. Moreover, from the first equation, we get $x_1=6x_2,$ which implies that the general solution is\n\n![](images/fa/fa0625bc99de3a27874b5695e72c9fd2e41ab618e49228086df7efcf7e1bb04f.svg)\n\nWe only require one eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}.$ Setting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the eigenvector\n\n![](images/bc/bcd87529387e7c400b7f5ccd896cfcbe3e42ab386a5b9d84b565f12752e2c4c3.svg) \n\n**Note:** If we set $x_2=2,$ we'll get a different eigenvector, namely\n![](images/83/83541fd42d19f0e3b17be1c36983d50d1382f36352168bce2d148126db7bf265.svg)\nNote that $\\mathbf w$ is also an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=3.$ In fact, we can choose any real $x_2 \\neq 0$ to generate an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda = 3.$ \n\n**Watch out!** In the case of $x_2=0,$ we get the zero vector, but this is not an eigenvector. Remember that eigenvectors must be non-zero vectors." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093441 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A$ with an eigenvalue $\\lambda$, the **eigenspace** $V_\\lambda$ can be defined as the set of all possible eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda$ plus the vector $\\mathbf{0}{:}$\n![](images/78/78c2c84d57ef00f32675c6d56fa91af39e0680eadf05ec266b0bdab9a57a8388.svg)\n\nNote that the eigenspace consists of *all* (not only non-zero) solutions of the equation $(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, the eigenspace can also be viewed as the *null space* of the matrix $A-\\lambda I,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nV_{\\color{red}\\lambda} = \\textrm{Null}(A - {\\color{red}\\lambda} I).\n$$\n\nWe also have the following theorem:\n\n*Eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues are always linearly independent.*\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/e3/e3c63b7efcb927079757e6f0e3e008a6f5038e11957524f51e1b1f45fdecd5f7.svg)\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-2$ and $\\lambda_2=-1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are \n\n![](images/1c/1c5df8239dae6efdd15a27498a9535b855004e3570c8eff46534993b9ec1e75f.svg) \n\nHere, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are linearly independent (they are not collinear).\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can be* (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent. For example, the vectors\n\n![](images/c3/c303fb73d6fc039bfa536d6f7de3103e774d913d2514c10c17d59fcf48d5bf00.svg)\n\nare collinear eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ above that correspond to $\\lambda_2=-1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092465 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
